{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And I, where do I come.</b> Why was Reuben more afraid than the rest of his brothers? On the contrary, Judah should have had more reason to fear than the rest of his brothers, for he was appointed over his brothers until that point. Rather, one may answer that since he had given his birthright to Joseph, he feared lest his father suspect him more than the rest of his brothers, because he hated him on account of the birthright, as it is written: ״בחללו יצועי אביו נתנה בכורתו ליוסף״ (when he defiled his father's bed, his birthright was given to Joseph). Another interpretation: because Joseph had told their father of the defilement that Reuben committed, as Rashi explained regarding ״ויבא יוסף את דבתם״ etc. — that he would say the sons of Leah were suspected of forbidden relations. Therefore Reuben was worried lest they say: because of the evil report he brought to their father, Reuben hated him."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:46.152163790Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}